# SignFlow configuration template.
# Copy the values you need into your process environment or into a
# secrets file kept OUTSIDE the project folder, which run-server.cmd loads
# automatically:
#   %SIGNFLOW_SECRETS_FILE%  when that variable is set, otherwise
#   %USERPROFILE%\signflow-secrets\mail-secrets.env
# A mail-secrets.env inside the project folder is NOT loaded; the launcher
# prints a warning asking you to move it.
# NEVER commit real values - this file contains placeholders only.

# --- Public URLs -----------------------------------------------------
# Canonical base for the links in ACCOUNT e-mails: confirm your address
# (<base>/verify-email?token=...) and reset your password. The account
# holder opens these in a browser, so it must be reachable FROM THEIR
# MACHINE:
#   http://localhost:8080    only works on this PC (local trial).
#   http://192.168.x.y:8080  works for people on the same office LAN.
#   https://<public-host>    required for anyone outside your network
#                            (port-forward or a tunnel to port 8080).
# A localhost value here does NOT produce localhost links: the server
# replaces the host with a LAN address of this PC that it detects at
# startup, so somebody on the same network can open the e-mail.
# Set SIGNER_PUBLIC_BASE_URL below to override that with a public address.
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
# The product name those e-mails sign off with ("Regards, ...").
# APP_NAME=SignFlow

# Base for account e-mail links, used verbatim (highest priority).
# Set it whenever the account holders are NOT on this PC's local network:
#   SIGNER_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://signflow.example.com
# Leave empty to let the server choose: a non-local APP_BASE_URL when you
# set one, else a LAN address detected at startup, else APP_BASE_URL with
# a warning. The address actually used is logged at startup and shown in
# the desktop app, so the operator always sees what recipients received.
SIGNER_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# --- PostgreSQL (backend only; the desktop app NEVER connects) ------
SIGNER_DB_ENABLED=true
SIGNER_DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/Signflow
SIGNER_DB_USERNAME=postgres
SIGNER_DB_PASSWORD=

# --- JWT signing (REQUIRED outside development) ----------------------
# High-entropy secret of at least 32 bytes, e.g. the output of
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Without it the production server refuses to start. In the 'dev' profile
# an empty value means a random per-process key (sessions end on restart).
SIGNER_SERVER_JWT_SECRET=
# Optional token lifetimes (defaults shown).
# SIGNER_SERVER_ACCESS_TOKEN_MINUTES=15
# SIGNER_SERVER_REFRESH_TOKEN_HOURS=12

# --- Website sessions (security Phase C; defaults shown) -------------
# The website (/web/**) authenticates with a server-side session stored in
# PostgreSQL, never with a bearer token. Requires database mode.
# The session cookie's name. The __Host- prefix makes the browser itself
# enforce Secure + Path=/ + no Domain, so the cookie cannot be set by a
# subdomain or over plain HTTP. Production REFUSES any other name; the dev
# profile overrides it with SF_SESSION_DEV so plain HTTP works locally.
# SIGNER_WEB_SESSION_COOKIE=__Host-SF_SESSION
# Production REFUSES false.
# SIGNER_WEB_SESSION_SECURE=true
# Sliding window: a session dies this long after its last request.
# SIGNER_WEB_SESSION_IDLE=PT30M
# Hard ceiling measured from sign-in, regardless of activity. Must be
# longer than the idle timeout or startup fails.
# SIGNER_WEB_SESSION_ABSOLUTE=PT8H
# The CSRF bootstrap has to create a session for every visitor (risk C9),
# so one that has not signed in expires much sooner. Must be positive and
# no longer than SIGNER_WEB_SESSION_IDLE. Signing in promotes it.
# SIGNER_WEB_SESSION_ANON_IDLE=PT5M

# --- Website accounts (security Phase D; defaults shown) -------------
# At most this many account e-mails (verification, password reset) per
# RECIPIENT per 15 minutes, on top of the per-address limit.
# SIGNER_WEB_MAIL_LIMIT=3
# Risk D1: sign-up writes a users row before the address is proved, so it
# gets its own far tighter budget per client address — an hour-long
# window, not the shared limiter's minute. Below 1, or a non-positive
# window, fails startup.
# SIGNER_WEB_SIGNUP_LIMIT=5
# SIGNER_WEB_SIGNUP_WINDOW=PT1H
# Risk D11: an account that never proved its address and was never used is
# deleted after this long. It never touches a disabled account, one that
# has been signed in to, or one that holds a signer slot or owns a
# document. Set PT0S to switch the sweep off and keep such rows for ever
# — it is the only destructive housekeeping here, so it has an off switch.
# SIGNER_WEB_UNVERIFIED_RETENTION=P7D

# --- Desktop launch from the website (Phase B; defaults shown) ------
# Oldest desktop build allowed to open a launch link. Pre-releases sort
# before their release (0.1.0-SNAPSHOT < 0.1.0), as in semantic versioning;
# raise this once release builds are installed.
# SIGNER_DESKTOP_MIN_VERSION=0.1.0-SNAPSHOT
# Development only (production refuses it): accept desktop builds that
# report their version as "unknown" (IDE runs, unfiltered builds).
# SIGNER_DESKTOP_ACCEPT_UNKNOWN_VERSION=false
# SIGNER_DESKTOP_TASK_MINUTES=30

# --- Sign-in protection (optional; defaults shown) -------------------
# Database mode: this many wrong passwords in a row from ONE CLIENT
# ADDRESS lock that address out of that account for LOCK_MINUTES (risk L3
# — it used to lock the account for everyone, which let anyone keep a
# known account locked out for the price of five requests).
# SIGNER_LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES=5
# ...and this many failures from all addresses together lock the account
# itself, as a backstop against guessing spread over many addresses. Must
# be >= MAX_FAILURES or the account-wide lock would fire first and the
# per-address one never could; the server refuses to start if it is lower.
# SIGNER_LOGIN_ACCOUNT_MAX_FAILURES=50
# SIGNER_LOGIN_LOCK_MINUTES=15
# Every mode: at most RATE_LIMIT sign-in requests per client address per
# RATE_WINDOW_SECONDS, then HTTP 429.
# SIGNER_LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT=20
# SIGNER_LOGIN_RATE_WINDOW_SECONDS=60

# --- Production transport (see docs/TRANSPORT-SECURITY.md) ----------
# Either Spring Boot TLS (SERVER_SSL_ENABLED=true + SERVER_SSL_KEY_STORE...)
# or a TLS-terminating reverse proxy with:
# SIGNER_TLS_OFFLOADED=true
# ...and then also (required; real client addresses for the sign-in limit):
# SIGNER_FORWARD_HEADERS_STRATEGY=native
# Desktop: the https:// address of the coordination server.
# SIGNER_SERVER_URL=https://signflow.example.com

# --- The desktop application -----------------------------------------
# README's configuration table covers the rest of the desktop settings
# (SIGNATURE_LOGIN_*, SIGNATURE_PKCS11_ENABLED, PKCS11_LIBRARY,
# PKCS11_SLOT_INDEX, SIGNATURE_VERIFICATION_MAX_FILE_BYTES). These three
# are not in it:
# Where to scan for the known PKCS#11 driver files (eTPKCS11.dll,
# gclib.dll) when PKCS11_LIBRARY is not set. Comma-separated, for tests
# and non-standard installs; the default is the Windows driver directory.
# PKCS11_AUTO_DETECT_DIRS=C:\Windows\System32
# The credentials the desktop presents to the coordination server. Like
# SIGNATURE_LOGIN_*, a shared deployment secret rather than per-user
# accounts — change both for any real deployment.
# SIGNATURE_SERVER_USER=coordinator
# SIGNATURE_SERVER_PASSWORD=coordinator

# --- Development only -------------------------------------------------
# The launchers run-app.cmd / run-server.cmd enable the 'dev' profile
# (mock token, default accounts, plain HTTP). Never in production.
# SIGNER_MOCK_TOKEN_KEYSTORE=C:\Users\you\.signflow\dev-mock-token.p12
# Passwords of the three accounts the 'dev' profile seeds. They exist
# ONLY under that profile — production seeds no accounts and refuses
# plaintext passwords outright.
# SIGNER_USER_ALICE=alice-pass
# SIGNER_USER_BOB=bob-pass
# SIGNER_USER_CHARLIE=charlie-pass

# --- Email: any SMTP provider; Gmail is only one option (sec 32-33) --
# console = account e-mail links (confirm address, reset password) printed
#           to the server CONSOLE, not the log file — the dev mailbox.
#           Outside the 'dev' profile the server refuses to start in it.
# smtp    = really delivered through the settings below. The launcher
#           refuses to start when HOST/USERNAME/PASSWORD are missing
#           instead of silently falling back to console mode.
# Lines may be written as KEY=VALUE or as bash-style "export KEY=VALUE".
SIGNER_EMAIL_MODE=console
SIGNER_EMAIL_FROM=no-reply@signflow.local
SPRING_MAIL_HOST=localhost
SPRING_MAIL_PORT=1025
SPRING_MAIL_USERNAME=
SPRING_MAIL_PASSWORD=
SPRING_MAIL_PROPERTIES_MAIL_SMTP_AUTH=true
SPRING_MAIL_PROPERTIES_MAIL_SMTP_STARTTLS_ENABLE=true

# --- Download fallback -----------------------------------------------
# Installer link offered when this server has no file of its own to serve
# (SIGNER_DOWNLOAD_DIR below is empty or holds nothing); leave empty to
# show an honest "distributed by your organisation" note instead.
SIGNER_DOWNLOAD_URL=

# --- The desktop installer -------------------------------------------
# The folder the published installer and its .sha256 are copied into. It
# must be OUTSIDE the jar and outside any directory the server publishes as
# static content: nothing under src/main/resources/static, because that IS
# the website. The server reads files out of here by name; the path itself
# never reaches a browser. See docs/desktop-download.md for how to publish.
#
# Leave empty and there is nothing to download from this server, and the
# website offers SIGNER_DOWNLOAD_URL above instead (or says the download is
# not ready yet). Set to a folder that does not exist and the server FAILS
# TO START, deliberately.
SIGNER_DOWNLOAD_DIR=C:\Users\you\signflow-releases
# The name the browser saves the file under when a signed-in reader presses
# Download for Windows on the dashboard. Letters, digits, dot, dash and
# underscore only, ending in .msi; anything else fails startup. The public
# /downloads/** route is unaffected and keeps the versioned name.
SIGNER_INSTALLER_FILENAME=SignFlow-Setup.msi
# Per client address, across all download routes together.
# SIGNER_DOWNLOAD_RATE_LIMIT=60
# SIGNER_DOWNLOAD_RATE_WINDOW=PT1M

# --- GitHub release mirror (optional; defaults shown) ----------------
# A second source for release files: the server can mirror the latest
# published GitHub release instead of relying only on the folder above.
# The source repository is private, so nothing here works anonymously —
# no token means the feature is OFF and the download folder remains the
# only source (the server logs which it is at startup). The token never
# reaches a log, a page or a download URL, and is stripped before
# following GitHub's redirect to the asset host, which needs no
# credential of its own. A read-only token is enough.
# GITHUB_API_TOKEN=
# GITHUB_RELEASE_REPOSITORY=CodeVoyager42-cyber/DesktopSign
# How long a release listing is cached before GitHub is asked again.
# GITHUB_RELEASE_CACHE_TTL=PT1H
# For tests and self-hosted proxies (GitHub Enterprise).
# GITHUB_API_BASE_URL=https://api.github.com

# --- Storage ---------------------------------------------------------
SIGNER_SERVER_STORAGE=C:\Users\you\signer-server-data

# --- Logging ---------------------------------------------------------
# SIGNER_LOG_FILE=C:\Users\you\AppData\Local\Temp\signer-app.log

# --- Document upload limit -------------------------------------------
# Largest PDF the desktop sends and the coordination server accepts, in
# bytes (default 100 MB). One value drives both the client-side check and
# the server's multipart max-file-size. Raise the request ceiling too when
# you raise the document limit (it covers the multipart envelope).
# SIGNER_UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=104857600
# SIGNER_UPLOAD_MAX_REQUEST_BYTES=115343360
